Subject: JSON.stringify writes primitive members without recursing and copies plain 8-bit strings in one piece

What this does
  - `json_is_prim` / `json_put_prim`: with no replacer, an int, double, bool, null or string member of an object (shape walk) or element of an array is written straight to the buffer, skipping `js_json_check` (which returns such a value unchanged when there is no replacer) and the recursive `js_json_to_str` call with its frame setup
  - `json_put_float64`: the double writer of `js_json_to_str`, moved out so both paths share it
  - `json_quote_str`: an 8-bit string with nothing to escape, going into an 8-bit buffer with room for it and both quotes, is checked with `json_scan_plain` and copied with one memcpy

Why we need it
  After 0588, `js_json_to_str` (per-value frame) and `json_quote_str` (a table test and a bounds-checked write per run) were half of stringify cycles on the app-traffic benchmark. json_app stringify only: -18.3% cycles, -14.5% instructions; whole json_app -3.8% cycles.

Soundness
  `js_json_check` only looks up toJSON for objects and BigInts, and calls the replacer only when there is one; for the five tags taken here with no replacer it returns the value itself, and `js_json_to_str` writes those tags exactly as `json_put_prim` does (same integer, double, literal and quoting code). Undefined, symbols, functions, ropes, objects and BigInts still take the general path. `json_scan_plain` with latin1 set stops at exactly the bytes the table escapes (quote, backslash, < 0x20); anything else falls back to the existing loop. Differentials identical old vs new: 19,938 stringify outputs (each special byte at every position of lengths 0-39, plain, in arrays, as keys, with gaps; buffer-room boundaries; edge numbers, replacers, property lists, getters that delete or change properties, holes, toJSON on Number/String prototypes, wrappers, wide strings, BigInt errors) and all earlier JSON differentials.

